{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-89660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n({4}^{x}+{x}^{2})(2x-3)\\ge 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-89660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since ${4}^{x}+{x}^{2}$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{({4}^{x}+{x}^{2})(2x-3)}{{4}^{x}+{x}^{2}} & \\ge \\frac{0}{{4}^{x}+{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2x-3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge 3 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-89660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d11eb9fa04c2a8ccdde789af0c913931499593e742a1fbe3e0d9ee432ec56115"} {"question" "q-89660" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-89660" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\ge 0"] ["math" "x\\ge 1"] ["math" "x\\ge \\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "x\\le -1"] ["math" "x\\le \\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-89660" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\ge \\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-271201" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n$\\frac{5-2x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-3)}\\ge 0,\\,x>2.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x\\le $\n{{field-3}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-271201" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $x>2,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & >4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3 & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-3)$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x>2).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-3),$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5-2x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-3)} & \\ge 0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-3))\\cdot \\frac{5-2x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-3)} & \\ge (\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-3))\\cdot 0 \\\\ 5-2x & \\ge 0 \\\\ -2x & \\ge -5 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign again of the inequality since we divided by a negative number ($-2$).\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x>2$ and $x\\le \\frac{5}{2}.$ So, the solution to our inequality is $2<x\\le \\frac{5}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-271201" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d11eb9fa04c2a8ccdde789af0c913931499593e742a1fbe3e0d9ee432ec56115"} {"question" "q-271201" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-271201" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-271201" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-271201" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "<"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-271201" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-271201" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-328551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n$(|x-2|+3)(4x-1)\\le 0.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-328551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the factor $|x-2|+3$ is always positive for all real numbers $x.$\n\nSince $|x-2|+3$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(|x-2|+3)(4x-1)}{|x-2|+3} & \\le \\frac{0}{\\,|x-2|+3\\,} \\\\ 4x-1 & \\le 0 \\\\ 4x & \\le 1 \\\\ x & \\le \\,\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-328551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d11eb9fa04c2a8ccdde789af0c913931499593e742a1fbe3e0d9ee432ec56115"} {"question" "q-328551" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-328551" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\le"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-328551" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-328551" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-328558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n$(-4{x}^{2}-1)(3x-2)<0.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-328558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $-4{x}^{2}-1$ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4{x}^{2}-1)(3x-2) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{(-4{x}^{2}-1)(3x-2)}{-4{x}^{2}-1} & >\\frac{0}{-4{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ 3x-2 & >0 \\\\ x & >\\,\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-328558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d11eb9fa04c2a8ccdde789af0c913931499593e742a1fbe3e0d9ee432ec56115"} {"question" "q-328558" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-328558" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math ">"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-328558" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-328558" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-330599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt{x+1}-3)(x-2)\\le 0,\\,x<8.\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x<$\n{{field-3}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-330599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $x<8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & <8 \\\\ x+1 & <9 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+1} & <3 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+1}-3 & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\sqrt{x+1}-3$ is negative for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x<8).$ So, we can divide both sides by $\\sqrt{x+1}-3,$ provided that we flip the sign of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{x+1}-3)(x-2) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1}-3)(x-2)}{\\sqrt{x+1}-3} & \\ge \\frac{0}{\\sqrt{x+1}-3} \\\\ x-2 & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x\\ge 2$ and $x<8.$ So, the solution to our inequality is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\le x<8\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-330599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d11eb9fa04c2a8ccdde789af0c913931499593e742a1fbe3e0d9ee432ec56115"} {"question" "q-330599" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-330599" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040172/state.json" "file_sha256" "e3696edabaa693b78003e9955a84eb580bfae812abb021e2004fef9f89c8c956"} {"question" "q-330599